findings.body:
`testDownloadingSVGImage_withKingfisherProcessor_forStandardSVGCase` removed both its `XCTestExpectation` and its `wait(for:)`, but it still calls `downloadImage` and asserts inside the completion handler. The test method returns as soon as `downloadImage` schedules the request. The XCTAssert calls then run after the test has ended, so a wrong size or wrong data will not fail this test. The sibling tests in the same file all keep their expectation.

::diff{file="BrowserKit/Tests/SiteImageViewTests/SVGImageProcessorTests.swift" lines="12-37"}

::callout{type="warning"}
This is a silent loss of coverage on the standard SVG path, which is the most basic case in the suite. Restore the expectation and the wait, as the other three tests do.

::suggestion{file="BrowserKit/Tests/SiteImageViewTests/SVGImageProcessorTests.swift" lines="26-36"}
        let exp = expectation(description: "Image download and parse")

        let siteDownloader = DefaultSiteImageDownloader()
        siteDownloader.downloadImage(with: mockedURL, options: [.processor(SVGImageProcessor())]) { result in
            switch result {
            case .success(let result):
                XCTAssertEqual(result.originalData, imageData)
                XCTAssertEqual(result.url, mockedURL)
                XCTAssertEqual(result.image.size, expectedRasterSize)
                exp.fulfill()
            case .failure(let error):
                XCTFail("Should not have an error: \(error) \(error.errorDescription ?? "")")
                exp.fulfill()
            }
        }
        wait(for: [exp], timeout: 2.0)

The PR deletes `@MainActor` from `startMockImageServer` and deletes the comment above it. That comment said `GCDWebServer` must be initialized for the first time on the main thread, and that the `@MainActor` guard keeps the file from crashing when run alone. The test methods are no longer `async` or main-actor-isolated, so nothing else now enforces that ordering. XCTest usually runs synchronous methods on the main thread, so this may not crash in practice, but the guarantee is gone and the reason for it is no longer recorded. If the suite is run in a different order or setup, the first `GCDWebServer` init can happen off the main thread.

::diff{file="BrowserKit/Tests/SiteImageViewTests/SVGImageProcessorTests.swift" lines="206-209"}

::callout{type="warning"}
Either keep `@MainActor` on the helper, or keep the original comment and state the invariant some other way. Removing it silently is the problem.

`static let generator` is now marked `nonisolated(unsafe)`, and the comment directly above it says `ContentBlockerGenerator` is not thread-safe. The annotation tells the compiler to trust that access is safe. That is an unchecked promise, and it hides the warning that would otherwise surface the problem. Today this is a single-run CLI `@main` entry point, so the practical risk is low. The problem is that the annotation turns a documented hazard into a silent one, and the PR description says there were no warnings, which suggests the annotation was added to clear one.

::diff{file="BrowserKit/Sources/ExecutableContentBlockingGenerator/MainContentBlockerGenerator.swift" lines="5-6"}

::callout{type="info"}
If the generator is only ever used from `main`, make that an explicit invariant, for example by creating it inside `main` or the entry method instead of as shared static state. Otherwise, keep the annotation and say in the comment why access is serialized.
